  conditions, outpatient care, and prior hospitalization for pneumonia than unvaccinated subjects. Unvaccinated         
  subjects, however, were more likely to have been given a prior diagnosis of dementia or stroke. Vaccination against   
  influenza was associated with a reduction in the risk of hospitalization for cardiac disease (reduction of 19         
  percent during both seasons [P<0.001]), cerebrovascular disease (reduction of 16 percent during the 1998-1999 season  
