  diagnostic code, and vaccination status to assess whether influenza was more likely than other infections to trigger  
  AMI. RESULTS Of 22,024 patients with acute coronary syndrome, 11,208 met the criterion of having had their first AMI  
  at the age of ≥ 40 years, and 3927 had also consulted for acute respiratory infection. AMI risks were significantly   
  raised during days 1-3 after acute respiratory infection (incidence ratio, 4.19 [95% confidence interval,             
  3.18-5.53], with the effect tapering over time. The effect was greatest in those aged ≥ 80 years (P = .023).          
